You didn't know? Hmm....

#pray is listed among the extended commands. It's rather easy to find out
      what it is for, you'll also learn very quickly not to use it too
      often. True, it's difficult to find what the exact timeout is, it's
      what you call experience ;-)
#offer is also a known extended command. At the first try you'll find that
      it's unuseable without altars, and on an altar it gives you a list of
      things you can sacrifice.
      That the altars can be used to distinguish cursed/blessed status of
      things is one of those casual discoveries that happen from time to time
      and make the game pleasant.
Polypiling - the big step here is to discover that polymorph affects objects
      as well as monsters. Once you know that (I learned that by zapping a
      dragon over some older corpse) and are bright enough you will know
      how to make use of this feature.
Unicorns: are described in the data file - just remember to ask about every
      monster by /. You'll learn about properties of their horns from there
      as well. Then you find that an unicorn horn is a tool - so you know how
      to use it... easy. About throwing gems - I discovered it myself as
      well, don't remember how (possibly from rumors).
Finally the aim of the game (amulet) is described in the first 10 lines
      of the guidebook, which you probably never cared to look at...

In summary - nethack is documened very well. What you need to know you can
either directly read, or discover within at most two years of frequent
playing. All you need is brains. No, we don't need any spoilers, thank you.
If you want a game in which you know everything from the very beginning
- try space invaders. Nethack is about discovering things.

